# Code readability

- Prefer the smallest reliable change that fits existing module boundaries and naming conventions.
  Reuse existing behavior before introducing helpers; extract shared policy when duplication would
  allow behavior to diverge. Avoid speculative abstractions, unused exports, dead branches, unused
  dependencies, and artifacts without a current consumer.
- Name values and functions by their domain meaning. Keep execution order visible; prefer guard
  clauses and straightforward control flow over nested conditions, clever expressions, and wrappers
  that only forward arguments. Split functions when responsibilities differ, not at an arbitrary size.
- Separate logical phases with one blank line: setup, validation, transformation, external effects,
  and result. Keep related declarations together, then leave a blank line before the next phase.
  Keep tightly related statements together; do not insert a blank line after every statement.
- Use braces for multi-line conditions and nontrivial branches. Comments explain a constraint or
  reason that code cannot express, rather than paraphrasing the next statement.
- Prettier owns mechanical formatting. These rules and code review own semantic grouping and
  understandability; do not enforce a blank line after every declaration through ESLint.
- Tests describe observable behavior with clear setup, action, and assertions. Keep fixtures minimal
  and use synthetic data. Apply these conventions to touched logic without unrelated mass reformatting.
